    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d427">

                <pb facs="0372" n="364" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg364"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1163 Am 3/237: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-05T15:00:00+08:00">October 5, 1937—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 5—12:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>760. Your [<hi rendition="#italic">Shanghai’s</hi>] 738, September 20, 11 p.m.
                    Commander-in-Chief’s 0019–2015<anchor n="86" xml:id="d427-f1"/> and Department’s
                    263 [<hi rendition="#italic">261</hi>] September 24, 1 p.m., to Nanking. Foreign
                    Office replying to Embassy protest of September <pb facs="0373" n="365"
                        xml:id="pg365"/> 21 denies that Chinese Air Force dropped bombs in Pootung
                    on 18th but states instructions have again been issued to various forces to pay
                    strict attention to foreign property in Shanghai.</p>
                <p>Sent to Shanghai, repeated to Department.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
